{"candidates":[{"content":"The category-error framing of 14e084ed5ca7 abstracts the layer-boundary distinction of 2edd20cc5e66: both share a mechanism—delivery metadata (delivery IDs, producer epochs, sequence numbers) as the invariant that enables duplicate detection—but the category-error framing collapses the writer-safety / reader-idempotency split into a single protocol-level property (distinguishability). This abstraction is refuted if the replay layer requires content-level semantics for correct deduplication (as anticipated by 14e084ed5ca7's own countercondition that distinguishability reduces to content idempotency when the replay layer ignores delivery metadata), which would force the two-layer model back into the description and show that distinguishability alone cannot replace the safety/idempotency distinction.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"14e084ed5ca745f165af78ba87bacf022eb02009f568e890426876a57e039e0e","quote":"This 'distinguishability' is neither idempotency nor safety; it is a protocol-level invariant that the log enforces through metadata (delivery IDs, producer epochs, sequence numbers)."},{"block":"2edd20cc5e66fc9a101c6281776b2f87205d239a7fa43391655c3ae100ba4e1b","quote":"safety is the property demanded of the producer (it may append duplicates, but they must be identifiable), while idempotency is the property demanded of the consumer/replay engine (processing a duplicate must not change derived state beyond the first processing)."}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"2edd20cc5e66fc9a101c6281776b2f87205d239a7fa43391655c3ae100ba4e1b"},{"role":"dependence","target":"bbf87d372e3f8753b8171e01fb95408cb4cea21b43bf6209bf8e72c436cdfc69"},{"role":"dependence","target":"14e084ed5ca745f165af78ba87bacf022eb02009f568e890426876a57e039e0e"}],"typicality":0.72}],"simulation_proposals":[]}